Q: Snowflake check if table exist A: <pre><code>CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE CHECK_TABLE_OR_VIEW_EXISTENCE(
    FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME STRING
)
RETURNS VARIANT
LANGUAGE SQL
EXECUTE AS OWNER
AS $$
/*  Validates that a fully-qualified object name resolves to either a base
    table or a view in Snowflake, and reports the resolved object type.

    The procedure verifies that the database component exists, that the object
    name follows the form db_name.schema_name.object_name, and that the object
    resolves uniquely to a supported object type. Validation is performed using
    INFORMATION_SCHEMA metadata and executes with owner privileges.

    Parameter
    ---------
    FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME
        Fully-qualified object name in the form
        db_name.schema_name.object_name.

    Behavior
    --------
    - Validates that FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME contains at least three name parts.
    - Verifies that the database component exists.
    - Resolves the object name using INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES.
    - Confirms that the object is either a BASE TABLE or a VIEW.
    - Returns the resolved object type on success.

    Return Value
    ------------
    A VARIANT object containing:
        FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME : the input object name
        status               : 'SUCCESS' or 'FAILURE'
        result               : on success, the resolved object type
                              ('BASE TABLE' or 'VIEW');
                              on failure, a descriptive error message

    Error Handling
    --------------
    Any validation or runtime error results in a failure status and an
    explanatory message in the return object. When failure cause is not
    identified otherwise, Snowflake SQLCODE and SQLERRM values are included.

    Revision History
    ================
    2026-04-08 Deke Eusey
    Original
*/
declare
    status                  varchar default 'FAILURE';
    default_failure_msg     varchar default 'Cause of failure is unidentified';
    failure_msg             varchar default default_failure_msg;
    result                  varchar default failure_msg;
    output                  variant default object_construct('status', status,
                                                             'result', result);
begin

    begin -- Begin block is followed by exception handler

        -- Parse FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME  -----------------------------------------
        LET db_name     VARCHAR := SPLIT(FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME, '.')[0];
        LET schema_name VARCHAR := SPLIT(FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME, '.')[1];
        LET object_name VARCHAR := SPLIT(FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME, '.')[2];
        IF (object_name IS NULL) THEN
            failure_msg := 'Argument to FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME does not have form db_name.schema_name.object_name';
            RAISE statement_error;
        END IF;

        -- Check if first qualifier refers to a database -----------------------
        LET db_name_invalid BOOLEAN := TRUE; -- Boolean variable holds true/false

        SELECT COUNT(*) = 0                  -- Result is true or false (Boolean value)
        INTO :db_name_invalid                -- Into Boolean variable (see LET above)
        FROM SNOWFLAKE.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.DATABASES
        WHERE   DATABASE_NAME   = :db_name
        ;
        IF (db_name_invalid) THEN            -- Test value (true or false) of Boolean variable
            failure_msg := 'Argument to FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME contains invalid database component';
            RAISE statement_error;
        END IF;

        -- Check if FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME refers to a table or view -------------
        LET info_tables_fqn STRING := db_name || '.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES';
        LET object_type STRING := 'Undetermined';

        SELECT LISTAGG(DISTINCT TABLE_TYPE,',')
        INTO :object_type
        FROM IDENTIFIER(:info_tables_fqn)
        WHERE   TABLE_SCHEMA    = :schema_name
                AND TABLE_NAME  = :object_name
        ;
        IF (object_type = '') THEN
            failure_msg := 'Argument to FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME does not resolve to a table or view';
            RAISE statement_error;
        END IF;
        IF (NVL(object_type NOT IN ('BASE TABLE', 'VIEW'), TRUE)) THEN
            failure_msg := 'object_type: '||NVL(object_type, 'NULL');
            RAISE statement_error;
        END IF;

        status := 'SUCCESS';
        result := object_type;

    exception
        when other then
            if (failure_msg=default_failure_msg) then
                failure_msg := 'SQLCODE='||:sqlcode||', SQLERRM='||:sqlerrm;
            end if;
            result := failure_msg;
    end;

    output :=
        object_construct(
            'FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME', FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME,
            'status', status,
            'result', result
        );

    return output;
end
$$;
</code></pre>
<pre><code>CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE CALL_CHECK_TABLE_OR_VIEW_EXISTENCE(
    FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME STRING
)
RETURNS VARCHAR(16777216)
LANGUAGE SQL
EXECUTE AS OWNER
AS $$
BEGIN
    LET src_check VARIANT;
    CALL ZZ_TST_INGEST.UTIL.CHECK_TABLE_OR_VIEW_EXISTENCE(:FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME) INTO :src_check;
    RETURN  'FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME='||src_check:FULLY_QUALIFIED_NAME
            ||', status='||src_check:status
            ||', result='||src_check:result;
END
$$;
</code></pre>

Q: I installed a custom method on “String.prototype”; how could it obtain the string it was called on? A: <p>You can't do exactly what you want because strings are immutable in javascript. It means that you can't change a string without creating a new one.</p>

<p>Depending on what you really need to achieve, you can use another object to "hide" the real string : </p>

<pre><code>var MyString = function(s) {
    this.s = s;
};

MyString.prototype.toString = function() {
    return this.s;
};

MyString.prototype.testMethod = function() {
   this.s += " reason why this is working";
};

var myVar = new MyString("some");
alert(myVar); // "some"
myVar.testMethod();
alert(myVar); // "some reason why this is working"
</code></pre>
